INV-bobor's filed root cause is false. It says the gate is path-triggered only;
an unconditional arm has lived in .woodpecker/full-suite.yml since 097ab4e05a,
forty-two minutes before the 63-commit window opened, and the fetched log for
the 2026-08-19 01:00 cron firing names the regression exactly:

    check-self-claims-drift: SELF-CLAIM DRIFT
      REGRESSED: runtime-cli-no-host-fs [verdict]:
        "confirmed_with_caveats" -> "violated"

The remedy the item proposes -- a scheduled arm alongside the path trigger --
was already shipped four days before the item was filed. Detection was never
the failure. Delivery was, three ways a second scheduled arm cannot fix:

  MASKING     the cron publishes ONE aggregate status for four unrelated gates.
              At the 2026-08-20 firing that status was failure while this gate
              PASSED in the same run; the red came from one intermittent tracker
              TUI assertion inside test-all-packages (filed WI-ruhul).
  ADDRESSING  a cron status attaches to whichever commit was tip at firing time,
              while scripts/ci-debug renders only HEAD. The instrument that
              answers "is dev green?" cannot see a cron verdict at all.
  ERROR       the run the item calls the gate's last is truncated mid-analysis
              with no verdict line, matching its `error` status. A pipeline that
              dies inside a gate is indistinguishable from one that passed it.

So the gate moves to where the merge path already reads it. auto-pr polls
exactly one context, so a gate in the per-PR pipeline bounds detection at ONE
commit by construction and needs no delivery mechanism.

The trade this reverses was deliberate and is re-priced, not overruled. The
comment justified claim-surface scoping at "~10 minutes" per PR; measured on dev
6e290fcdb8 the gate is 3m49s, and in CI it shares build-grammars with -- and
runs concurrently with -- a pytest step that took 935s in the same pipeline.
The decision was taken against a number 2.6x too high.

The trigger becomes *code_paths plus docs/hypergumbo-self-catalog/**, which
holds a README that *code_paths does not match; kept explicit so a security
gate's trigger cannot narrow by accident again.

tests/test_ci_self_claims_gate_scope.py pins the widened trigger, keeps the
cron arm unconditional, and asserts both arms run the SAME command block --
woodpecker.yml claimed in prose that they "cannot disagree on identical trees"
and nothing enforced it. It imports yaml HARD rather than via importorskip:
pyyaml is a declared hypergumbo-core dependency present in every test container,
so importorskip could only ever produce a silent skip. Positive control run
before committing: reverting the trigger to the narrow list fails the guard.

Masking and addressing are filed as their own invariant (INV-bozid) -- they
apply to self-tree-validation, test-agent-infra and all four nightly matrix
legs, not just this gate.

